      "settings": {
        "heading": "Bodology for Weight:\nThe Studies",
        "subtext": "Weight is metabolic before it's behavioural. When cells respond poorly to insulin, the body stores more and burns less — and blood sugar crashes drive hunger. The evidence below is a chain with two links: dips make you eat more, and inositol supports how your body handles glucose and insulin.*"
      }
    },
    "link_1_hunger": {
      "type": "study-overview-table",
      "blocks": {
        "attr_participants": { "type": "attribute", "settings": { "label": "1,070 participants" } },
        "attr_meals": { "type": "attribute", "settings": { "label": "71,715 real-world meals" } },
        "attr_cgm": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "CGM-tracked",
            "popup_title": "Continuous Glucose Monitors",
            "popup_text": "Every participant wore a continuous glucose monitor (CGM), so researchers saw real blood sugar curves after real meals — not lab questionnaires. Two cohorts (UK and US) produced directionally consistent results."
          }
        },
        "attr_population": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Healthy adults",
            "popup_title": "Who Was Studied",
            "popup_text": "This study was in healthy adults — not specifically women with PCOS. It maps how blood sugar dips drive hunger in the general population."
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["attr_participants", "attr_meals", "attr_cgm", "attr_population"],
      "settings": {
        "anchor": "link-1",
        "title": "Link 1 — Blood sugar dips make you hungry",
        "show_image_mobile": true,
        "background_heading": "The Study",
        "background_text": "Wyatt et al., 2021, in Nature Metabolism: 1,070 people wore glucose monitors through 8,624 standardised meals and 71,715 freely-chosen ones. The size of the blood sugar dip 2–3 hours after a meal predicted hunger better than the spike did. Bigger dips meant more hunger (r = 0.16), a shorter wait until the next meal, more calories at 3–4 hours (r = 0.19), and more calories over the whole next day (r = 0.27) — all p < 0.001. The spike isn't the problem. The crash is.¹",
        "attributes_heading": "Study Attributes"
      }
    },
    "link_2_inositol": {
      "type": "study-overview-table",
      "blocks": {
        "attr_insulin": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "−47% fasting insulin*",
            "popup_title": "Fasting Insulin, −47%",
            "popup_text": "In a randomized, placebo-controlled 6-month trial of the 40:1 blend in 46 women with PCOS, average fasting insulin fell from 20.19 to 10.74 μU/mL — with no significant change on placebo (Benelli et al., 2016).*"
          }
        },
        "attr_postmeal": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Post-meal insulin ↓*",
            "popup_title": "Post-Meal Insulin",
            "popup_text": "In a randomized head-to-head ratio trial, post-meal (postprandial) insulin improved significantly from baseline after 3 months of inositol (Nordio et al., 2019). Post-meal is exactly the 2–3 hour window where Wyatt's blood sugar dips predict hunger.*"
          }
        },
        "attr_homa": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "−42% HOMA index*",
            "popup_title": "HOMA Index, −42%",
            "popup_text": "The HOMA index estimates insulin resistance from fasting glucose and insulin. In Benelli 2016, it fell 42% on average over 6 months on the 40:1 blend (3.38 → 1.97) — with no significant change on placebo.*"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["attr_insulin", "attr_postmeal", "attr_homa"],
      "settings": {
        "anchor": "link-2",
        "title": "Link 2 — Inositol supports insulin & glucose handling",
        "show_image_mobile": true,
        "background_heading": "The Evidence",
        "background_text": "Across the randomized trials on this page and our PCOS page, inositol consistently improved how the body handles insulin: fasting insulin fell 47% and the HOMA insulin-resistance index fell 42% over 6 months on the 40:1 blend (Benelli, 2016²) — and in a head-to-head ratio trial, both fasting and post-meal insulin improved after 3 months (Nordio, 2019³). Post-meal is the exact window where the hunger-driving dips happen.*",
        "attributes_heading": "The Numbers"
      }
    },
