            "popup_title": "Three Groups, Same Diet",
            "popup_text": "Group 1: diet alone (n = 21). Group 2: diet + 4 g myo-inositol daily (n = 10). Group 3: diet + myo and D-chiro-inositol in the 40:1 ratio (n = 12). Everyone followed the same calorie-controlled diet and took 400 µg folic acid daily."
          }
        },
        "attr_random": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Randomized",
            "popup_title": "Randomized",
            "popup_text": "Part of a gold-standard study, \"randomization\" means assigning participants to groups based on chance, to compare outcomes fairly and minimize bias."
          }
        },
        "attr_ratio": {
          "type": "attribute",
          "settings": {
            "label": "40:1 ratio",
            "popup_title": "The 40:1 Ratio",
            "popup_text": "40:1 is the physiological ratio of myo-inositol to D-chiro-inositol in blood plasma — the same ratio used in Bodology. Group 3 took 1.1 g myo + 27.6 mg D-chiro daily."
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["attr_duration", "attr_participants", "attr_arms", "attr_random", "attr_ratio"],
      "settings": {
        "anchor": "study-1",
        "title": "Study 1 Overview",
        "show_image_mobile": true,
        "background_heading": "Study Background",
        "background_text": "An independent randomized trial asking a precise question: does adding inositol to a diet change what kind of weight you lose? 43 overweight women with PCOS followed the same calorie-controlled diet for 6 months — alone, with myo-inositol, or with the 40:1 myo + D-chiro blend (Le Donne et al., 2019).⁴",
        "attributes_heading": "Study Attributes"
      }
    },
